                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "The core loop is far too shallow to be called a strategy game: there's a map, but you never need to move anywhere to win; a battle system, but fighting actively hurts you more than it helps since you lose population either way; and builders/soldiers that turn out to be entirely optional busywork, since the castle levels itself up automatically from random events. Players note there's effectively one difficulty setting (trivially easy) and the whole thing can be finished by just clicking \"advance\" repeatedly with no real decisions required. It's also widely compared to the developer's own earlier \"Ant War\" game reskinned with a castle theme, and to the original 2004 Age of Castles it's supposedly based on, with much less depth than either. No achievements on top of that compounds the sense of a lazy, low-effort re-release rather than a genuine remake. Actual meaningful choices between building, fighting, and expanding — where each has real risk and reward — would go a long way toward making this a real strategy game instead of a glorified idle-clicker in castle skin.",
